kantCondT -- SEQ 675 (Tony's ruling of 2026-11-17), the kant jitter's step 4: native compare, if and while, the all-or-nothing cut.
An if or a while runs natively only when its condition is a compare the kind pass has marked and every statement in every
arm or body is native: a store of step 3b's kind, or another native if or while, to any depth. Anything else in it sends
the whole statement to testOP, as before. A compare used as a VALUE (x = a < b) is not native: false is nothing.
pop.sh runs this file twice: as written, and with the top-level kwCompile( and kwRan( lines deleted (the twin, all
interpreted). Stdout from KC ROW1 to KC END must be equal.

    M1 (stderr, interpreted, pinned as read): what a statement hands back, read by running testOP on an action's last
       statement after the ones before it. An if whose arm is one store hands back that store's target field (hqT, hqET,
       hqEF, hqO); one whose arm is a block hands back the block's own statement list (hqB, hqEB), which is not the
       action's list; an if that runs no arm hands back nothing (hqF); a while hands back nothing, after zero passes or
       some (hqW0, hqWN). addrOf prints its own name for nothing. The native text hands %last the same things.
    M2 (stderr, pinned in genLadder/kantCondT.m2.txt): how the build nests them. An if is an instruct, op ifThen, whose one
       operand is the list opArms: condition, then-statement, else-statement when there is one. An `or` arm is the same
       instruct sitting in the else slot (hqO). A block arm is an instruct, op seq (bodySeq inside a loop), whose operand
       is its statement list (hqEB, hqWN). A while is op whileLoop with opArms: condition, body.
    ROW1   the six compares < <= > >= == != as conditions, on two counts, two numbers, and a count with a number both ways.
    ROW2   M3: the six compares where a side is nan or inf (step 3c's / makes them). Interpreted, a number compare is the
           sign of the difference, and a difference that is nan counts as equal: so nan against anything, and inf against
           inf, answer false true false true true false. The number spellings in incant/setup say the same (fcmp ueq, ule,
           uge, one; olt and ogt as they were).
    ROW3   if; if-else; elseif, each arm taken and none taken; block arms; an if in an if in an if with a mixed compare.
    ROW4   while with zero, one and many passes; on numbers; a count against a number; a loop in a loop; an if-else in
           a loop; a loop in an if.
    ROW5   two actions called twice.
    ROW6   the fall-backs, each still on testOP (KC NATIVE prints 1 or 0 per top-level statement):
           fqP a print in the loop body; fqB a break in the body; fqV a compare as a value, and the bare field it is
           tested by; fqR a local read in one if that only an earlier if wrote (the first if is native, the second is
           not: a store inside a block counts for nothing after the block ends); fqC a bare field as the condition.
    KC RAN <action> <statements run> testOP <calls>: the text's own counts, top-level statements only. A native if or
           while is one statement and no testOP call; nothing is counted inside it.
    KC TEXT: wqW's text (a loop in a loop), pinned in genLadder/kantCondT.wqW.ll. A go-on check follows every native store.
Action names here avoid the jitter's own local names (kiT, kfP, kwN ...): a fixture action of the same name is found in
their place and the run goes wrong in silence at exit 0 (met while this file was written).
